From 147a269e589b77476bafba4501ab3eb4a8724f8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Destrudos6 <3411622425@qq.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 14:58:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] Overhaul the translation pipeline following proven
 open-source tools

Lessons borrowed from pyvideotrans (19k stars) and YouDub-webui
(5.7k stars), the two most battle-tested open-source subtitle
translators:

- Sentence fragments are joined before translating. Auto-generated
  subtitles use rolling windows that cut one spoken sentence into
  several cues; translating them separately produced incoherent
  bilingual lines. A cue whose text does not end a sentence now merges
  with the following cue when they are at most 1.5s apart and the
  combined text fits one subtitle line (80 chars). Cache version
  bumped to 7 so existing caches regenerate.
- Translations are aligned by echoed line number instead of array
  index. A reply that skips or reorders a line can no longer shift
  every following translation by one unnoticed; unknown, duplicated
  or missing numbers simply count as untranslated lines and go through
  the existing re-translation path.
- A reply the provider truncated (finish_reason other than "stop") is
  treated as a failed batch, not silently accepted as-is.
- HTTP 4xx replies (except 408/429) abort the whole translation via a
  shared stop flag instead of burning three retries on every batch;
  the video is not marked done, so it can be retried after fixing the
  key or model name.
- The prompt now spells out what the strong tools rely on: exact 1:1
  count with echoed numbers, consecutive-lines context, zero-shift
  fragment translation, concise colloquial phrasing fit for one
  on-screen line, and a prompt-injection guard treating input lines
  as data.
- Batches are additionally split at 8000 characters (except the
  explicit whole-video request) so a batch of long lines cannot
  exceed the model's output limit.
